id: 1152
event: message.updated
data: {"type": "message.updated", "sequence": 1152, "run_id": "run_7d89fb6062b44bd8", "data": {"id": "3485662e-6db5-4640-ae76-3f95946e256e", "run_id": "run_7d89fb6062b44bd8", "kind": "tool", "position": 1148, "revision": 1152, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "agent_call_id": null, "data": {"name": "Read", "input": "{\"file_path\": \"[workspace]\", \"limit\": 20, \"offset\": 120}", "output": "120\t\n121\tThe eigenphase sum increases monotonically from **−1.11 rad to +0.47 rad** over 0.5–30.0 eV, with two inflection points at **eKE ≈ 2.5 eV and 17.0 eV** (identified by sign changes in the second derivative). The initial rapid rise (derivative up to 0.14 rad/eV) slows after ~17 eV, consistent with saturation behavior.\n122\t\n123\tThe angle-averaged delay mirrors the Wigner delay shape: min = −104.09 as at 0.9 eV, max = 141.36 as at 8.6 eV.\n124\t\n125\t**Core quantitative result:** Wigner delay range = [−21.7, +182.6] as; zero crossings at 2.9 and 17.1 eV. Eigenphase sum range = [−1.11, +0.47] rad; inflection points at 2.5 and 17.0 eV. Analysis unit: per-energy-point curve evaluation.\n126\t\n127\t**Supporting evidence:**\n128\t- `output/fig4_wigner_delay.csv` — full Wigner delay curve\n129\t- `output/fig4_angle_averaged_delay.csv` — angle-averaged delay\n130\t- `output/fig4_eigenphase_sum.csv` — eigenphase sum\n131\t- `output/deep_analysis_results.json` — feature locations\n132\t- `output/analysis_02_core_findings.py` — analysis code lines 230–290\n133\t- `output/analysis_04_deep.py` — zero-crossing and extremum detection\n134\t\n135\t**Alternative explanations:** (1) The zero crossings and inflection points are characteristic of single-channel resonant scattering but could also arise from multi-channel threshold effects. (2) The large delay values (up to 182 as) suggest a metastable state or shape resonance in this energy range. (3) Without theoretical predictions for comparison, the microscopic origin of these features cannot be uniquely assigned.\n136\t\n137\t**Limitations:** The Wigner delay and eigenphase sum cover a different energy range (0.5–30 eV) than the angle-resolved data (22.5–50 eV), limiting direct cross-correlation. The 0.5 prefix in `0.5_Wigner_delay` denotes a specific theoretical approximation; its relationship to the full Wigner delay is unspecified.\n138\t\n139\t**Falsification proposal:** Calculation of the energy derivative of the eigenphase sum (Ramsauer-Townsend relation) and comparison with the Wigner delay zero-crossing positions would test whether the features correspond to actual resonance phases.", "status": "completed", "is_async": false, "tool_use_id": "call_8c9b3f17511d4c88a03783f9"}, "created_at": "2026-10-16T09:24:44.125542+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-16T09:24:44.710898+00:00"}}

